<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jerusalem</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jerusalem is an ancient city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, sacred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. It was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, restored under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in the seventh century. Its religious politics under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> sparked the Maccabean revolt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s recapture of it in 1187 defined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerusalem.jpg" alt="Jerusalem" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godot13 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Mount_(Aerial_view,_2007)_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…population of the Kingdom of Israel, the &quot;Ten Lost Tribes&quot; of biblical tradition, marked the history of Israel and Judah deeply; the Assyrian crisis is reflected in the prophetic books of Isaiah, Hosea and Micah, and in the biblical account of Sennacherib's siege of <b>Jerusalem</b> in 701 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Saladin's nephew, Sultan al-Kamil, later handed <b>Jerusalem</b> to Emperor Frederick II by treaty in 1229, in return for help against his own Ayyubid relatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The capture of <b>Jerusalem</b> in 597 BCE, which led to the deportation of King Jehoiachin and the Judean elite, and the destruction of the city and its Temple after a second siege in 587/586 BCE, showed that the army could sustain operations far from home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He fought at Antioch and <b>Jerusalem</b>, and from 1102 besieged Tripoli, building the castle of Mont Pèlerin outside it; he died there in 1105, and the city fell in 1109 to his son Bertrand, founding the County of Tripoli.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Crusade took <b>Jerusalem</b> in 1099, and the last Levantine stronghold, Ascalon, fell in 1153.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Georgian Orthodox Church was the largest landowner after the crown, and Georgian monasteries flourished abroad, notably the Iviron on Mount Athos, the Monastery of the Cross in <b>Jerusalem</b> and Petritsoni in Bulgaria, linking the kingdom to the wider Orthodox world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
